WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:04.920
For more than a decade, Dennis Franz was one
of the most recognizable faces on television

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:07.840
thanks to his role as Andy Sipowicz on NYPD
Blue.

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:13.560
But since the series wrapped in 2005, he hasn't
appeared in a single movie or TV show.

00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:14.460
Why?

00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:17.820
Here's the real reason you don't hear from
Dennis Franz anymore.

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:24.780
From 1993 to 2005, Dennis Franz anchored NYPD
Blue, portraying Andy Sipowicz for a good

00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:27.060
chunk of the character's life, as well as
his own.

00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:31.920
Twelve years is a long time to hold a job,
and by the end of it, Franz had had enough.

00:00:32.120 --> 00:00:35.380
Instead of moving on to another project, he
decided to just retire.

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:36.860
He told The New York Post,

00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:38.140
"I was just tired.

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:42.059
It was becoming a job, as much as I loved
the work and the challenges and the creative

00:00:42.059 --> 00:00:46.770
process...I just wanted to live an enjoyable,
irresponsible, spend-time-with-my-family kind

00:00:46.770 --> 00:00:47.260
of life.

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:49.060
I haven't regretted one minute of it.

00:00:49.420 --> 00:00:50.680
I'm pretty good at doing nothing."

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:54.450
Franz and his wife now spend a lot of time
at their lake house in northern Idaho when

00:00:54.450 --> 00:00:56.040
they aren't traveling around the world.

00:00:56.700 --> 00:00:59.140
Sounds a lot better than tracking down killers
every week!

00:00:59.580 --> 00:01:03.780
Andy Sipowicz might be one of the most famous
TV cops of all time, but the character was

00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:07.720
far from the first law enforcement officer
Franz had played in his long career.

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:13.260
In fact, Franz told the Los Angeles Times
that by his own count, Sipowicz was the 28th

00:01:13.270 --> 00:01:16.850
police officer he had portrayed
across television, movies, and theater.

00:01:16.850 --> 00:01:20.780
He was so typecast that he almost didn't take
the NYPD Blue gig at all.

00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:23.280
"I told myself I was going to try to find
another vehicle.

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:25.500
It was time not to play any more cop roles."

00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:28.060
It's no wonder he decided to retire!

00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:30.140
"How's it feel?"

00:01:31.900 --> 00:01:33.300
"Takes some getting used to."

00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:38.540
Before teaming up with legendary TV writer-producer
Steven Bochco on Hill Street Blues and NYPD

00:01:38.540 --> 00:01:42.801
Blue, among others, Franz made his name in
film as a regular for two of the most most

00:01:42.801 --> 00:01:48.940
important and singular directors of the 1970s
and 1980s: Brian De Palma and Robert Altman.

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:53.560
Franz appeared in five De Palma movies during
the director's peak: The Fury, Dressed to

00:01:53.560 --> 00:01:55.580
Kill, Blow Out, Scarface, and Body Double.

00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:59.680
He also joined Altman's ensemble films A Wedding,
Popeye, and The Player.

00:01:59.940 --> 00:02:04.820
Sadly, though, Altman passed away in 2006,
while Bochco passed in 2018.

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:08.680
And while De Palma is still active, his output
has slowed dramatically.

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:13.400
It's understandable if Franz would rather
stay retired rather than go it alone without

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:14.980
any of his biggest creative partners.

00:02:16.030 --> 00:02:20.040
Though he hasn't appeared on screen in years,
Franz has had his fair share of real life

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:20.540
drama.

00:02:20.820 --> 00:02:26.080
For instance, in 2007, a huge chandelier fell
from the ceiling in Franz's house in Idaho,

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:27.480
injuring his sister Marlene.

00:02:28.120 --> 00:02:32.360
The two sued for negligence, with Franz revealling
that as a result of the accident, he was forced

00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:37.490
to forgo a $16 million personal services contract,
which suggests fans might have missed out

00:02:37.490 --> 00:02:40.620
on a possible comeback project thanks to the
faulty chandelier.

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:46.400
And in 2010, Franz was involved in another
lawsuit, calming he was owed nearly $70,000

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:50.120
by a charter jet company for services he paid
for but never received.

00:02:50.300 --> 00:02:53.480
Luckily, they were able to come to an agreement
before the matter went to trial.

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:59.030
Dennis Franz hasn't acted on screen since
2005, so fans were pleasantly surprised when

00:02:59.030 --> 00:03:02.160
he made an unexpected appearance at the 2016
Emmys.

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:06.300
It sparked rumors of a possible comeback,
something that Franz told the New York Post

00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:07.480
is still a possibility.

00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:11.040
"My agents have an understanding; they're
still gonna knock on my door periodically

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:15.230
to see if there are any home runs to get me
off my butt, but so far that hasn't presented

00:03:15.230 --> 00:03:19.480
itself, though I left my options open and
didn't say I was, for sure, closing the door

00:03:19.480 --> 00:03:20.800
and not leaving it open a crack."

00:03:21.160 --> 00:03:23.760
Here's hoping someone offers him a juicy role
sometime soon.

00:03:24.040 --> 00:03:25.980
Just… no more cops, okay?

